(Abstract) Tj
T*
(Background: TB treatment non-adherence threatens individual outcomes and public health) Tj
T*
(through emergence of drug-resistant strains. Digital adherence technologies combined with) Tj
T*
(objective biomarker verification may improve outcomes. Objective: To evaluate whether) Tj
T*
(integrated digital technology \(TB-TST\) plus standard care improves TB treatment completion) Tj
T*
(compared to standard care alone. Methods: A total of 68 TB patients \(mean age 38 years;) Tj
T*
(64% male\) in Argentina's public TB program were randomized to standard directly observed) Tj
T*
(therapy \(n=34\) or standard care plus TB-TST mobile app with urine metabolite monitoring) Tj
T*
(\(n=34\). Primary endpoint was TB treatment completion at 6 months. Secondary endpoints) Tj
T*
(included metabolite-confirmed adherence rates, app engagement metrics, and patient) Tj
T*
(satisfaction. Results: Treatment completion occurred in 76% of TB-TST participants versus) Tj
T*
(56% of standard care \(relative risk 1.36; 95% CI 1.01-1.82; P = 0.039\). Metabolite testing) Tj
T*
(confirmed adherence on 87% of scheduled assessments in the intervention arm. App) Tj
T*
(utilization was high \(mean 68% of prescribed daily reminders accessed\). Patients reported) Tj
T*
(improved confidence in their adherence \(P = 0.008\) and strengthened therapeutic) Tj
T*
(relationships with treatment supporters. No serious adverse events occurred. Conclusion:) Tj
T*
(Integrated digital adherence technology with metabolite verification enhances TB treatment) Tj
T*
(completion in pragmatic clinical settings, supporting scale-up for TB program) Tj
T*
(implementation.) Tj
